    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "np34aju",
      "content": "<img></img>The Panchayati Raj System represents rural local self-government in India. It aims to decentralize power and ensure people's participation in governance at the grassroots level. The system received constitutional status through the 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Constitutional Part",
          "value": "Part IX",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Governance Model",
          "value": "Three-Tier Structure",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Base Level Body",
          "value": "Gram Sabha",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "District Level Body",
          "value": "Zila Parishad",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "cym6c9a"
    },
    {
      "id": "ym24cuf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Background of Panchayati Raj"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Constitutional provisions governing local self-government in India are located under Part IX.",
        "The Panchayati Raj System is organized as a Three-Tier Structure across village, block, and district levels.",
        "The Gram Sabha acts as the foundational unit of direct participation in local governance.",
        "Executive local self-governance is divided among the Gram Panchayat (Village), Panchayat Samiti (Block), and Zila Parishad (District)."
      ],
      "id": "3jcr4xc"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8yud6ok",
      "content": "• Village self-government existed in ancient India."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lm0ipi8",
      "content": "• The Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957) recommended democratic decentralization."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tbdorru",
      "content": "• The committee suggested a three-tier Panchayati Raj system."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zgljkyr",
      "content": "• The 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992 gave constitutional status to Panchayats."
    },
    {
